{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And if you reject My statutes</b> — Because the statutes are commandments whose reason has not been revealed to the public, the fools will reject them; they will say: what does it matter to HaShem whether or not I wear this garment woven with embroidery of fine linen and tekhelet? And what does it avail that we burn the cow and sprinkle its ashes upon us? But as for the ordinances, everyone desires them and everyone needs them — there can be no settlement for a people or a state without law. And no person's soul abhors an ordinance such as \"one who strikes a man and he dies\" (Exodus 21:12), \"and when men strive\" (ibid. v. 22), and the laws of the ox, the pit, the bailees, and similar matters. But the law carried out against transgressors of commandments — such as a man who lies with forbidden relations, or who profanes the Sabbath, or who makes a familiar spirit and a wizard — at this they bristle, because the commandments are a heavy yoke upon the wicked. Therefore it says, \"and if your soul abhors My ordinances, so as not to do all My commandments\" — for abhorrence of the ordinances is for the purpose of not performing the commandments." "And the meaning of <b>to break My covenant</b> is that the covenant will be nullified entirely, and they will be without Torah, permitting themselves forbidden relations in public, and every desire that pleases them. And by way of truth, this covenant will be nullified and abolished, the opposite of \"and I will give peace in the land\" (Leviticus 26:6). And this is what it says: \"who have broken My covenant, though I was a husband to them\" (Jeremiah 31:31) — because they annulled My covenant of peace, I became a husband against them in their very selves."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "I resolved הלהמון as לההמון ('to the public'), reading the prefixed bet as le-.", 1 "I rendered בעלתי בם hyper-literally; Ramban plays on the dual meaning of בעל as 'husband/master' and 'lord over,' exploiting the paradox that HaShem's covenantal marriage turns into dominance."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:55.546991756Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}